1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:07,274
Can you show me ReplicaSets?

2
00:00:07,833 --> 00:00:12,508
A ReplicaSet keeps a given number of identical Pods running.

3
00:00:12,508 --> 00:00:18,040
You say "3 Pods like this"; it counts the Pods that match its selector,

4
00:00:18,040 --> 00:00:22,870
and creates or deletes Pods until the count is right, forever.

5
00:00:22,870 --> 00:00:23,493
Analogy:

6
00:00:23,493 --> 00:00:24,506
a thermostat.

7
00:00:24,506 --> 00:00:31,908
You set 21 °C; it does not heat once, it keeps measuring and corrects whenever the room drifts.

8
00:00:32,467 --> 00:00:36,382
Lesson 06 showed that a deleted Pod never comes back.

9
00:00:36,382 --> 00:00:40,961
One Pod is also one point of failure and has a fixed capacity.

10
00:00:40,961 --> 00:00:46,132
Applications need several copies (replicas) for availability and load,

11
00:00:46,132 --> 00:00:50,416
and they need something that replaces a copy when it dies.

12
00:00:52,167 --> 00:00:54,586
And how does it actually work?

13
00:00:55,133 --> 00:01:01,048
The controller manager (lesson 02) runs a loop for every ReplicaSet:

14
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:02,980
1.

15
00:01:02,980 --> 00:01:05,499
The ReplicaSet and its Pods:

16
00:01:06,033 --> 00:01:06,191
1.

17
00:01:06,191 --> 00:01:08,391
The ReplicaSet and its Pods:

18
00:01:08,391 --> 00:01:13,185
The Pod names are the ReplicaSet's name plus a random suffix.

19
00:01:13,185 --> 00:01:13,343
2.

20
00:01:13,343 --> 00:01:14,364
Self-healing:

21
00:01:14,364 --> 00:01:15,386
delete a Pod.

22
00:01:15,386 --> 00:01:19,473
🧹 This deletes one of the three web Pods on purpose:

23
00:01:20,033 --> 00:01:24,900
The Pod names are the ReplicaSet's name plus a random suffix.

24
