00:01:35,167 --> 00:01:38,076
How do I see what Kubernetes did?

30
00:01:38,633 --> 00:01:41,168
Every Pod records who owns it:

31
00:01:42,933 --> 00:01:44,522
Let me try something.

32
00:01:44,522 --> 00:01:49,063
A ReplicaSet whose selector does not match its own template:

33
00:01:49,600 --> 00:01:51,356
What should happen?

34
00:01:51,356 --> 00:01:53,204
A second ReplicaSet.

35
00:01:53,204 --> 00:01:54,498
What happened?

36
00:01:54,498 --> 00:01:59,120
The API server refused it before storing anything.

37
00:02:00,867 --> 00:02:07,966
Make the template's labels match the selector (here, by applying a corrected copy through standard input):

38
00:02:09,700 --> 00:02:10,413
Got it.

39
00:02:10,413 --> 00:02:13,165
What do I need to remember?

40
00:02:13,700 --> 00:02:18,251
A ReplicaSet keeps replicas Pods matching its selector running:

41
00:02:18,251 --> 00:02:19,189
self-healing.

42
00:02:19,733 --> 00:02:26,078
The selector must match the template's labels; Pods are counted by labels, not names.

43
00:02:26,633 --> 00:02:33,063
A ReplicaSet never updates existing Pods; Deployments manage ReplicaSets to do updates.

44
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:39,977
I keep hearing about Deployments, rolling updates and rollbacks.

45
00:02:39,977 --> 00:02:41,514
What is it, really?

46
00:02:42,067 --> 00:02:48,000
A Deployment is the standard way to run a stateless application on Kubernetes.

47
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:52,108
It manages ReplicaSets (lesson 08), which manage Pods.

48
00:02:52,108 --> 00:02:55,455
When you change the Deployment (a new image,

49
00:02:55,455 --> 00:03:01,845
a new setting), it creates a new ReplicaSet and moves the Pods over to it gradually:

50
00:03:01,845 --> 00:03:03,138
a rolling update.

51
00:03:03,138 --> 00:03:06,789
It keeps the old ReplicaSets, so it can go back:

52
00:03:06,789 --> 00:03:07,626
a rollback.

53
00:03:08,167 --> 00:03:10,311
Lesson 08 ended with a problem:

54
00:03:10,311 --> 00:03:16,054
a ReplicaSet never updates its Pods, and replacing them all at once means downtime.

55
00:03:16,054 --> 00:03:19,374
Real applications are updated many times a week.

56
00:03:19,374 --> 00:03:22,349
A Deployment updates them without downtime,

57
00:03:22,349 --> 00:03:30,651
stops when something goes wrong, records the history of versions and lets you return to a previous one with one command.

58
00:03:32,400 --> 00:03:34,819
And how does it actually work?

59
00:03:35,367 --> 00:03:35,515
1.

60
00:03:35,515 --> 00:03:41,213
The Deployment controller creates a new ReplicaSet from the changed template.

61
00:03:41,213 --> 00:03:41,361
2.

62
00:03:41,361 --> 00:03:47,800
It scales the new one up and the old one down, a few Pods at a time, within two limits:

63
00:03:47,800 --> 00:03:48,466
maxSurge:

64
00:03:48,466 --> 00:03:55,127
how many Pods above replicas may exist during the update (extra capacity); maxUnavailable:

65
00:03:55,127 --> 00:03:58,531
how many Pods below replicas may be not ready.

66
00:03:58,531 --> 00:03:58,679
3.

67
00:03:58,679 --> 00:04:04,526
A new Pod counts only once it is ready (its readiness probe passes, lesson 14).

68
00:04:06,267 --> 00:04:06,429
1.

69
00:04:06,429 --> 00:04:13,746
Deploy version 1.0.0 and record why (the change-cause annotation shows up in the history):

70
00:04:14,300 --> 00:04:14,463
1.

71
00:04:14,463 --> 00:04:21,779
Deploy version 1.0.0 and record why (the change-cause annotation shows up in the history):

72
00:04:22,333 --> 00:04:22,478
2.

73
00:04:22,478 --> 00:04:24,721
Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pods:

74
00:04:24,721 --> 00:04:30,508
The Pod names carry the ReplicaSet's name, which carries a hash of the template.

75
00:04:30,508 --> 00:04:30,653
3.

76
00:04:30,653 --> 00:04:37,308
